-- title:
--   Generic linear form for an ideal of maximal growth
-- statement:
--   Let $n,m$ be natural numbers with $1\le m$, let $k$ be an infinite field, and write $S=k[x_0,\dots,x_n]$. For an ideal $I\subseteq S$ and $d\in\mathbb N$, `piece I d` denotes the quotient of the space of degree-$d$ forms by those lying in $I$, i.e. $(S/I)_d$, and [`Nat.macaulayPow m`](def/Nat_MacaulayPow.html#L7) is the Macaulay bound $a\mapsto a^{\langle m\rangle}$. Assume: $J\subseteq S$ is generated by a set of forms all of degree $m$; $\dim_k(S/J)_{m+1}=\bigl(\dim_k(S/J)_m\bigr)^{\langle m\rangle}$; and $\iota:Z\to\operatorname{Proj}S$ is a closed immersion such that for every $d\ge m$ and every form $F$ of degree $d$, $F\in J$ if and only if for each $i$ the pullback along $\iota$ of the section of $\mathcal O$ on $D_+(x_i)$ given by the homogeneous localisation $F/x_i^{d}$ vanishes. Then there is $a\in k^{n+1}$, with $\ell:=\sum_j a_jx_j$, such that: (i) for each $i$, multiplication by the pullback of $\ell/x_i$ on $\Gamma(Z,\iota^{-1}D_+(x_i))$ kills only $0$; and (ii) for every ideal sheaf datum $\mathcal I_\ell$ on $\operatorname{Proj}S$ whose ideal on each affine chart $D_+(x_i)$ is spanned by the sections $G/x_i^{d}$ with $G$ a degree-$d$ form in $(\ell)$, and every closed immersion $\iota':Z'\to\operatorname{Proj}S$ with $\ker\iota'=\ker\iota\sqcup\mathcal I_\ell$, the ideal $J'$ generated by the degree-$m$ forms in $J+(\ell)$ satisfies $\dim_k(S/J')_{m+1}=\bigl(\dim_k(S/J')_m\bigr)^{\langle m\rangle}$, satisfies the same chartwise vanishing criterion in degrees $\ge m$ with respect to $\iota'$, and obeys $\dim_k(S/J')_m<\dim_k(S/J)_m$ whenever $\dim_k(S/J)_m>0$.
--
--   This is the inductive step for cutting an ideal of maximal growth by a general hyperplane: the three conclusions say that a generic linear form is a non-zero-divisor on the structure sheaf of $Z$ chart by chart, that the degree-$m$ truncation of $J+(\ell)$ again has maximal growth and cuts out the scheme-theoretic intersection $Z\cap V(\ell)$ in degrees $\ge m$, and that the numerical invariant $\dim_k(S/J)_m$ strictly decreases. It feeds the vanishing and global-section statements used in the Hilbert-functor constructions.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicGeometry_ProjSpace_exists_linearForm_section_maximal_growth.lean

